SELF STUDY QUESTIONS
1. What is the difference between “sample” data and “population” data? Why does this matter for the purposes of #descriptivestats?

   Answer: Sample data refers to the data collected on a subset of the population while the population data refers to the data including each individual member of the whole population of interest. For example, if you are collecting population data on the price of coffee in Hyderabad, you will have to collect the data from all cafes in the city. Most of the time, it is impossible, or too resource-intensive, to obtain population data. Therefore, you can do convenience sampling and visit cafes in your neighborhood and obtain sample data instead.  
   
   This is important for #descriptivestats because we need to know which type of data our descriptive statistics are summarizing. The calculation might even be different depending on whether we are working with sample or population data; this is true for the standard deviation). In statistics, it’s conventional to use different notation depending on the type of data, adopting lowercase letters for sample statistics (e.g., x̄, s) and Greek letters for population parameters (e.g., µ, σ).

2. What are some examples of “measures of location”? How are they helpful?

   Answer: Measures of location can also be referred to as measures of central tendency. They include mean, median, and mode. They are can be used to understand what a typical member of the dataset looks like and where the data tends to cluster. The mean adds up all the data points and divides the sum by the total number of data points. The median is the middle value of a dataset. The mode is the value that occurs most frequently in a dataset.

3. What are some examples of “measures of spread”? How are they helpful?

   Answer: Measures of spread, such as range or standard deviation, tell us how spread or dispersed the dataset is. We can use measures of spread to estimate the likelihood of extreme values and the shape of the distribution. 
   -   Range  shows the difference between the highest and lowest values. It’s a helpful quantity to show the span of possible values a variable takes on, but it might be misleading as a measure of spread when there are outliers. The majority of data could be closely clustered, but one outlier could drastically impact the value of the range. The interquartile range can be used in such cases which is more robust against outliers. 
   -   Standard deviation  quantifies the amount of variability or spread in a dataset according to how much the data points deviate from the mean. To find this quantity, the deviations from the mean are squared, averaged, and then square-rooted. A smaller standard deviation indicates that the values in the dataset are closer to the mean, implying less variability. Conversely, a large standard deviation indicates a more dispersed distribution.

4. What is skewness? Describe what left and right skew looks like in a distribution.

   Answer: Skewness determines the extent to which a distribution deviates from being symmetric. Skewness can be quantified using different formulas, but a good rule of thumb is (mean - median) / (standard deviation). 
   
    Positive Skewness (Right Skew): A positively skewed distribution has a longer tail on the right side, meaning the distribution is stretched towards higher values. In this case, the majority of the data points are concentrated on the left side of the distribution, while a few larger values pull the mean towards the right. The skewness value would be greater than zero.
   
   Negative Skewness (Left Skew): A negatively skewed distribution has a longer tail on the left side, meaning the distribution is stretched towards lower values. In this case, most of the data points are concentrated on the right side of the distribution, while a few smaller values pull the mean towards the left. The skewness value would be less than zero. 
   
   Distributions with a positive skew tend to have mean > median > mode, while the opposite is true for a negatively skewed distribution. While this rule of thumb holds true for many unimodal distributions, it may not hold true for all. A simple counterexample is when there is a right skew, but the majority of cases are less than or equal to the mode, which is also the median. In this case, the mean would be located at the heavier left tail. Here, the mean is not greater than the median or mode. Fig.1 Example of a counterexample for the rule of thumb (Hippel, 2005)
   More explanations and details on skewness, including different ways to quantify it, can be found here: Statistics How To  and Wikipedia.

5. How are descriptive statistics displayed in histograms?

   Answer: A histogram shows how a quantitative variable in a dataset is distributed. It is a visual representation of several descriptive statistics, including mean, median, skew, standard deviation, range, and skew. In particular, the mode can sometimes be estimated by identifying the peak or the highest bar. The mean could be estimated by visualizing the center of mass or the balancing point of the distribution. The median can be estimated from the histogram by trying to visualize the point where half of the area is to the left and half of the area is to the right. 
   
   It is crucial to remember that a few descriptive statistics alone are not sufficient to fully infer the exact shape of the distribution. A full histogram is needed to know the shape of the distribution.

