- This HC is applicable whenever you are using statistics to understand a particular context. For example, in economics courses, in order to understand inequality in a particular country, you may look at the average income, quantiles, and skew of the income distribution.

APPLYING THIS HC OUTSIDE MINERVA
- This HC can be applied in various ways when interpreting statistics presented in news articles, advertisements, and all sorts of media. Not only is it important to understand what descriptive statistics can tell you, it’s crucial to understand what they don’t tell you. One must keep in mind the common ways in which descriptive statistics are misinterpreted or used to suggest misleading conclusions.   - Suppose that you are seeking out a new job, and you notice a low starting salary of $30,000. However, the employer tells you that the average salary within the company is $150,000, and you may get a raise later on. This information might be misleading. There could be a single outlier CEO earning $500,000 a year, which is skewing the distribution to the right, while in reality, no workers are earning more than $100,000. Using the principles of #descriptivestats, and recognizing the limitations of one isolated statistic, you might consider asking for the mode or the median wage.

